  {"id":53838,"slug":"stemonitis-splendens","sci":"Stemonitis splendens","common":"chocolate tube slime","family":"Stemonitidaceae","obs":92,"regions":["north-cascades","olympics","oregon-cascades"],"months":[5,6,7,8],"photos":[{"id":449139813,"ext":"jpeg","by":"emiliano_yguazu","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":215281560,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Nick Furlan","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":406554072,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Johan Adler","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":297688758,"ext":"jpg","by":"Daniel Zucker","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":244479929,"ext":"jpg","by":"Елена Солодовник","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Stemonitis splendens","summary":"Stemonitis splendens, commonly known as the chocolate tube slime, is a species of slime mold."},
  {"id":244531,"slug":"brefeldia-maxima","sci":"Brefeldia maxima","common":"Tapioca Slime Mold","family":"Amaurochaetaceae","obs":85,"regions":["olympics"],"months":[10,11,12],"photos":[{"id":108481360,"ext":"jpeg","by":"John Plischke","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":159213467,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Kayleigh Hummel","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":159891106,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Kayleigh Hummel","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":53790579,"ext":"jpeg","by":"ktfrogface","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":98535036,"ext":"jpg","by":"Clark","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Brefeldia maxima","summary":"Brefeldia maxima is a species of non-parasitic plasmodial slime mold, and a member of the Myxomycetes class. It is commonly known as the tapioca slime mold because of its peculiar pure white, tapioca pudding-like appearance. A common species with a worldwide distribution, particularly in North America and Europe. It is often found on bark after heavy rain or excessive watering."},
  {"id":408301,"slug":"trichia-varia","sci":"Trichia varia","common":"","family":"Trichiaceae","obs":78,"regions":["olympics"],"months":[3,10,11],"photos":[{"id":352530972,"ext":"jpg","by":"Alison Pollack","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":173319554,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Ryan Durand","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":232696037,"ext":"jpg","by":"Marcin Klisz","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":326557865,"ext":"jpg","by":"Aidan Hersh","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":232695886,"ext":"jpg","by":"Marcin Klisz","lic":"CC BY-NC"}],"wiki":"Trichia varia","summary":"Trichia varia is a type of slime mold in the order Trichiida. Its sporangium measures 0.6 to 0.9 millimeters, and its stalk 0.1 to 0.5 millimeters. It is a pale orange color, distinguished by other members of its genus by having two spiral bands on its elaters. It is distributed throughout various parts of the world."},
  {"id":56565,"slug":"comatricha-nigra","sci":"Comatricha nigra","common":"","family":"Amaurochaetaceae","obs":74,"regions":["north-cascades"],"months":[1,2,3,4,5,6,7,10,11,12],"photos":[{"id":176600140,"ext":"jpg","by":"Alexis Tinker-Tsavalas","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":176600092,"ext":"jpg","by":"Alexis Tinker-Tsavalas","lic":"CC BY"},{"id":4128090,"ext":"jpg","by":"Stu's Images","lic":"CC BY-SA"},{"id":60399528,"ext":"jpeg","by":"Bogdan Mazur","lic":"CC BY-NC"},{"id":4128087,"ext":"jpg","by":"Stanislav Krejčík","lic":"CC BY"}],"wiki":"Comatricha nigra","summary":"Comatricha nigra is a widespread species of slime molds in the family Amaurochaetaceae. It grows on decayed wood on the forest floor. The sporangium reaches a total height of 2–8 mm, while the black, hair-like stalk is usually two to six times the length of the sporangium. The color of the sporangium varies as a function of its stage of development, from translucent white, to pink, bright red, and eventually black."},
